@startuml
!include https://raw.githubusercontent.com/proveo-ca/identity/main/proveo.puml
skinparam noteTextAlignment left
' purpose: can a proveo sandbox run sbx inside itself, and can the image do it
' covers: defs/claudecode/mcp/Dockerfile
' covers: defs/base/Dockerfile
' Why it came up: E1 in sbx-kit-capabilities.puml is host-blocked; the ladder
' cannot be climbed from inside a sandbox, so the question is whether it could be.
' Notes here are capped at 5 lines x 50 columns; one idea per anchor.
'
' ── THE SHORT ANSWER ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
' No image change can do this, and defs/claudecode/mcp/Dockerfile is the wrong
' file to edit. /dev/kvm is a DEVICE NODE supplied by the runtime; an image
' layer cannot ship one. Everything the image CAN do (install the sbx CLI, add
' the user to a kvm group) is inert without the device.
'
' ── SOURCES ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
'   sbx install reqs : https://docs.docker.com/ai/sandboxes/install/
'   Arm install guide: https://learn.arm.com/install-guides/sbx/
'   Apple nested virt: https://developer.apple.com/documentation/virtualization
'                      (M3+ and macOS 15 Sequoia; M1/M2 excluded)
'
' Docker states the condition plainly, and it is a condition rather than a ban:
'   "If you're running inside a virtual machine or virtual desktop
'    infrastructure environment, the environment must support NESTED
'    VIRTUALIZATION."
' Arm's guide adds the blunt version: "KVM does not typically work on virtual
' machines. It requires bare metal."
'
' ── MEASURED INSIDE A LIVE proveo SANDBOX, 2026-09-06 ───────────────────────
' Four independent blocks, in the order they would have to fall:
'
'   1. NO DEVICE.  /dev is a 65 MB tmpfs carrying 15 nodes — core fd full mqueue
'      null ptmx pts random shm std{err,in,out} tty urandom zero. No kvm, and
'      no image layer can add one: /dev is mounted at runtime.
'
'   2. NO CAPABILITY.  CapEff is 0000000000000000 — the agent container holds
'      ZERO capabilities. Without CAP_MKNOD nothing can create the node even as
'      root, and without CAP_SYS_MODULE nothing can insert a driver.
'
'   3. NO MODULE.  /lib/modules/7.0.12 is empty and lsmod shows no kvm. There is
'      no kvm_arm64 to load into the microVM's kernel even given the capability.
'
'   4. NO CLI.  Nothing in defs/ fetches sbx. It is a HOST tool that creates
'      microVMs, and no proveo image has ever shipped it.
'
' Note the shape: a sandbox is a CONTAINER INSIDE A MICROVM, so 1-3 are the
' microVM's kernel and the container's capability set — two layers, neither of
' which the Dockerfile reaches.
'
' ── WHAT WOULD ACTUALLY HAVE TO CHANGE ──────────────────────────────────────
' In order, and only the last row is ours:
'
'   host CPU      Apple Silicon M3 or later — M1/M2 cannot, same ARMv8.6-A
'                 extension but nested virt is restricted to M3-series
'   host OS       macOS 15 Sequoia or later
'   sbx guest     the microVM kernel must carry kvm_arm64
'   sbx runtime   must expose /dev/kvm into the agent container, which today
'                 runs with an empty capability set
'   proveo image  install the sbx CLI  <- the only row a Dockerfile can do
'
' The middle two are sbx features that do not exist as far as the CLI reference
' documents: no --device, no --privileged, no --cap-add is published for
' `sbx run`. So this is an upstream request (docker/sbx-releases), not a
' packaging task.
'
' ── RECOMMENDATION: DO NOT BUILD IT ─────────────────────────────────────────
' The ladder is a HOST measurement and that is not a defect. E1 was verified on
' the host in minutes; making it verifiable in-sandbox would need a chip
' upgrade, an OS floor, two sbx features and an image change — to reach the same
' answer. Recorded so the next reader does not re-derive the chain.
'
' If it is ever wanted, the first gate is cheap and host-side:
'   sysctl -n machdep.cpu.brand_string     # must read M3 or later
'   sw_vers -productVersion                # must be 15.x or later
' Both green, and the question becomes an sbx feature request rather than a
' proveo one.
' ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

title Experiment — nested sbx: what would have to change, and where

component "host CPU\nApple Silicon M3+ · macOS 15+" as CPU <<cloud>>
component "sbx on the host\ncreates the microVM" as SBX <<cloud>>

node "proveo sandbox" as BOX <<host>> {
  component "microVM kernel 7.0.12\nno kvm module · empty /lib/modules" as KERN <<error>>
  component "agent container\nCapEff 0x0 · /dev tmpfs, 15 nodes" as CT <<error>>
  component "proveo image\ndefs/claudecode/mcp/Dockerfile" as IMG <<app>>
}

component "sbx CLI\nthe only row an image can add" as CLI <<db>>

CPU ARROW_MAIN SBX : nested virt, if M3+
SBX ARROW_MAIN KERN : would have to carry kvm_arm64
KERN ARROW_MAIN CT : would have to expose /dev/kvm
CT ARROW_ERROR IMG : zero caps — cannot mknod
IMG ARROW_QUEUE CLI : inert without the device

note bottom of CT
  **Zero capabilities is the hard floor.** CapEff
  reads 0000000000000000, so even root inside the
  container cannot mknod the device or insert a
  module — the image is arguing with the runtime,
  and the runtime does not lose.
end note

note bottom of IMG
  **A Dockerfile cannot ship a device node.** /dev
  is mounted at runtime as a 65 MB tmpfs, so a
  layer that creates /dev/kvm is overwritten
  before the first process runs.
end note

note bottom of KERN
  **The guest kernel has no kvm to load.**
  /lib/modules for 7.0.12 is empty and lsmod shows
  none, so the module path is closed before the
  capability question is even reached.
end note

note bottom of CPU
  **The floor is a chip, not a flag.** M1 and M2
  share the ARMv8.6-A extension but Apple limits
  nested virt to M3 and later, on macOS 15+, so
  no configuration reaches it below that.
end note

note bottom of SBX
  **Two of the five rows are upstream.** No
  --device, --privileged or --cap-add is published
  for sbx run, so exposing KVM is a feature ask
  rather than something proveo can package.
end note

@enduml
